":4,"k":1,"t":"Theory and Examples 41. Area Find the area of the region bounded above by y = 2 cos x and below by y = sec x, -p>4 … x … p>4."},{"x":0.7085,"y":0.23724,"w":0.08961,"h":0.01615,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"x72x4 + 1 dx."},{"x":0.53926,"y":0.26999,"w":0.34887,"h":0.01153,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"50. Using different substitutions Show that the integral"},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.27635,"w":0.41592,"h":0.0256,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"42. Volume Find the volume of the solid generated by revolving the region in Exercise 41 about the x-axis."},{"x":0.68171,"y":0.29049,"w":0.14322,"h":0.01918,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"((x2 - 1)(x + 1))-2>3dx"},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.30773,"w":0.41145,"h":0.02619,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"43. Arc length Find the length of the curve y = ln (cos x), 0 … x … p>3."},{"x":0.56545,"y":0.32629,"w":0.33329,"h":0.01151,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"can be evaluated with any of the following substitutions."},{"x":0.56627,"y":0.3348,"w":0.10981,"h":0.02091,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"a. u = 1>(x + 1)"},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.33971,"w":0.41144,"h":0.02619,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"44. Arc length Find the length of the curve y = ln (sec x), 0 … x … p>4."},{"x":0.56544,"y":0.3527,"w":0.38062,"h":0.02091,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"b. u = ((x - 1)>(x + 1))k for k = 1, 1>2, 1>3, -1>3, -2>3,"},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.37227,"w":0.41225,"h":0.0256,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"45. Centroid Find the centroid of the region bounded by the x-axis, the curve y = sec x, and the lines x = -p>4, x = p>4."},{"x":0.58588,"y":0.37555,"w":0.04453,"h":0.01212,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"and -1"},{"x":0.56708,"y":0.39003,"w":0.28629,"h":0.01555,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"c. u = tan-1 x d. u = tan-1 2x"},{"x":0.56709,"y":0.40258,"w":0.269,"h":0.02091,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"e. u = tan-1 ((x - 1)>2) f. u = cos-1 x"},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.40425,"w":0.41225,"h":0.0256,"fs":0.01151,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"46. Centroid Find the centroid of the region bounded by the x-axis, the curve y = csc x, and the lines x = p>6, x = 5p>6."},{"x":0.56626,"y":0.42584,"w":0.09918,"h":0.01555,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":0,"t":"g. u = cosh-1 x"},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.43409,"w":0.41223,"h":0.02773,"fs":0.01151,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"47. The functions y = ex3 and y = x3ex3 do not have elementary anti- derivatives, but y = (1 + 3x3)ex3 does."},{"x":0.53926,"y":0.44779,"w":0.22163,"h":0.01151,"fs":0.01151,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"What is the value of the integral?"},{"x":0.08775,"y":0.51092,"w":0.25207,"h":0.03581,"fs":0.03581,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"8.2 Integration by Parts"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.55688,"w":0.4625,"h":0.01279,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Integration by parts is a technique for simplifying integrals of the form"},{"x":0.62782,"y":0.58884,"w":0.07612,"h":0.01281,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ƒ(x)g(x) dx."},{"x":0.36259,"y":0.61953,"w":0.59307,"h":0.02815,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"It is useful when ƒ can be differentiated repeatedly and g can be integrated repeatedly without difficulty. The integrals"},{"x":0.72968,"y":0.66237,"w":0.04476,"h":0.01727,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"x2ex dx"},{"x":0.55736,"y":0.66685,"w":0.12112,"h":0.01281,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":0,"t":"x cos x dx and"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.69614,"w":0.59301,"h":0.0449,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"are such integrals because ƒ(x) = x or ƒ(x) = x2 can be differentiated repeatedly to become zero, and g(x) = cos x or g(x) = ex can be integrated repeatedly without diffi- culty. Integration by parts also applies to integrals like"},{"x":0.7061,"y":0.75879,"w":0.07238,"h":0.01422,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"ex cos x dx."},{"x":0.55328,"y":0.76021,"w":0.10293,"h":0.01281,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"ln x dx and"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.79024,"w":0.59303,"h":0.04417,"fs":0.01279,"n":3,"k":1,"t":"In the first case, ƒ(x) = ln x is easy to differentiate and g(x) = 1 easily integrates to x. In the second case, each part of the integrand appears again after repeated differentiation or integration."},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.85834,"w":0.2286,"h":0.01375,"fs":0.01375,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"Product Rule in Integral Form"},{"x":0.36258,"y":0.88298,"w":0.4521,"h":0.01281,"fs":0.01279,"n":1,"k":1,"t":"If ƒ and g are differentiable functions of x, the Product Rule says that"},{"x":0.52776,"y":0.90453,"w":0.25865,"h":0.0305,"fs":0.01279,"n":2,"k":1,"t":"d dx 3ƒ(x)g(x)4 = ƒ′(x)g(x) + ƒ(x)g′(x)."}]}